.Sh DESCRIPTION
These APIs define explicit section-to-segment mappings for generated ELF files.
Segments are attached to an
.Vt elfobj_t
and can then be used during ELF program-header emission.
.Pp
.Fn elf_segment_get
returns the segment at
.Fa index
in program-header order, and
.Fn elf_segment_section
the section at
.Fa index
among a segment's, in the order they were added.
.Fn elf_segment_split
moves the section at
.Fa index ,
which must be neither the first nor past the last, and the ones after it
to a new segment of the same type, flags and alignment that comes right
after
.Fa segment
among the program headers; a linker uses it once addresses are known, to
begin a segment where the sections stop being one run of memory.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Creation functions return a segment handle on success or NULL on failure.
.Fn elf_segment_get
and
.Fn elf_segment_section
return NULL for an index past the last, and
.Fn elf_segment_split
the new segment, or NULL for an index out of range, an object that can no
longer change, or no memory.
Mutation functions return
.Dv ELF_OK
on success or an error code.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Add a loadable program segment to a generated object and inspect it.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <elfobj.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int
main(void)
{
    elfobj_t *obj = elf_init_i386();
    elf_segment_t *seg;

    if (obj == NULL)
        return 1;

    seg = elf_add_load_segment(obj, 0x5 /* PF_R|PF_X */, 0x1000);
    if (seg == NULL) {
        elf_close(obj);
        return 1;
    }

    printf("segment type=%u flags=%#x align=%llu\en",
        elf_segment_type(seg), elf_segment_flags(seg),
        (unsigned long long)elf_segment_align(seg));

    elf_close(obj);
    return 0;
}
